 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "THE FLORAL CLOCK. A working clock made from thousands of plants, laid out afresh every spring, and the oldest in the world. It keeps good time. It also keeps its hands, very tidily, in the border."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 16,
   "text": "THE SCOTT MONUMENT. Sir Walter Scott's Gothic spire, 61 metres tall and the biggest monument to a writer anywhere. There are 287 steps to the top. Scott wrote about forty books. The steps are fewer than the pages."
  },
  {
   "x": 33,
   "y": 11,
   "text": "PRINCES STREET GARDENS. Once the Nor' Loch, a muddy lake under the Castle. It was drained, and the gardens were laid out in the early 1800s. It's far more peaceful now. The lake monsters left."
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "EDINBURGH CASTLE. It sits on Castle Rock, the stump of a volcano that went quiet about 340 million years ago. The Crown Jewels of Scotland and the Stone of Destiny live inside. Every day but Sunday, the One O'Clock Gun is fired at one o'clock. People set their watches by it. The gun sets nothing by anyone."
  },
  {
   "x": 27,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE ESPLANADE. A flat parade ground in front of the Castle, where the Royal Edinburgh Military Tattoo is held every August. A lot of pipers, drummers and dancers. A lot of tartan, too, worn in the proper way."
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 40,
   "text": "THE ROYAL MILE. About a mile long, from the Castle to Holyrood, and made of closes, wynds, pubs, shops and old stairs. Underneath it lie buried streets, like Mary King's Close, closed up and built over. It's like a city that decided to keep a very old diary."
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 42,
   "text": "HOLYROOD. The King's home in Scotland. Mary, Queen of Scots, lived here, and so did a choir of very shiny crowns. At the foot of the Mile, it's the end of the road and the beginning of the park."
  },
  {
   "x": 36,
   "y": 33,
   "text": "AULD REEKIE. Edinburgh's old nickname, from the smoke of chimneys when the Old Town was packed in tight. It's far fresher now. The nickname stuck. Nicknames do."
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "D\u00d9N \u00c8IDEANN. The Gaelic name of this city, 'the fort of Eidyn'. It's pronounced about like 'doon AY-dinn'. Say it with a straight face and a few locals will take you aside to say it better."
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 51,
   "text": "THE GRASSMARKET. Once a cattle market under the Castle, now a square of pubs, shops and tartan scarves. Greyfriars Bobby, the loyal little Skye terrier who guarded his master's grave for fourteen years, is a short walk away. Everyone loves Bobby. Even the cats."
  },
  {
   "x": 57,
   "y": 49,
   "text": "ARTHUR'S SEAT. A hill made from an extinct volcano, 251 metres high, right beside the city. It's an easy hour's walk to the top, with views of the whole of Edinburgh, the Forth and, on a good day, a smug seagull who got there first."
  }
 ],
